1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Qd2 O-O 8.
f4 c5 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. g3 Nb6 ({A new move, first played by Black in 1937
against T. Tylor. It seems better than the usual} 10... a6) 11. O-O-O {Based
on the idea that if Black continues with 11...c4, then 12.Bg2 threatening g4
and f5 and Black will have to move the knight from b6 in order to start a pawn
attack with b7-b5.} cxd4 ({If} 11... Nc4 {at once,} 12. Bxc4 dxc4 13. d5 {
with the better position.}) 12. Nb5 ({If} 12. Nxd4 {at once,} Bd7 {. If then}
13. Ncb5 Nxd4 14. Qxd4 Bxb5 15. Bxb5 Rfc8 {threatening Nc4.}) 12... Bd7 13. Bd3
{This seems to lose time.} ({If} 13. Nd6 f6 14. Nxd4 fxe5 15. Nxc6 bxc6 ({if} 
15... Qxd6 16. Nxe5) 16. fxe5 {and Black has the open b- and f-files.}) (13. h4
{could have been played first, to which Black could reply} Qc5) (13. Kb1 {
could also be played.}) 13... Nc4 (13... Qc5 14. Nfxd4 Nxd4 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 {
loses to} 16. Bxh7+) ({if} 13... Nb4 14. Nd6 {, for if} Nxa2+ 15. Kb1 {wins
the knight.}) 14. Bxc4 dxc4 15. Nfxd4 ({This is now necessary to stop c4-c3,
although White might perhaps have risked} 15. Nd6 {, threatening Nxc4, since
after the text move, Black gets a free game and an attack.}) 15... Nxd4 16.
Nxd4 Rfd8 {Preventing 17.Nf5.} 17. Qc3 {This invites a pawn attack. 17.Qa5 or
17.Qe3 was better.} Rac8 (17... Rdc8 {threatening a5, seems strong. If then}
18. Qa5 Rc5) 18. a3 Qc5 19. Qb4 (19. Rhe1 {at once was better, to threaten g4
and f5.}) 19... Qc7 20. Rhe1 a5 21. Qc3 b5 22. b4 Ra8 ({If} 22... axb4 23. Qxb4
) ({or} 22... cxb3 23. Qxc7 Rxc7 24. Nxb3) 23. Kb2 Rdb8 ({Overlooking White's
reply.} 23... Ra7 {defending the queen and freeing 24...Ra8, or 23...Qb7, was
better.}) 24. Nb3 axb4 25. axb4 Bc6 26. Nc5 Bd5 27. Ra1 ({If} 27. f5 Qxc5 28.
bxc5 b4 29. Qe3 c3+ 30. Kb1 Ra2 31. Rxd5 Rb2+ {wins.}) 27... Qc6 28. Rxa8 Rxa8
29. Ra1 Rxa1 30. Kxa1 Qa8+ 31. Kb2 Kf8 32. Qe3 ({To keep queen and knight
which, according to Dr. Lasker, is better than queen and bishop. White first
intended} 32. Qa3 Qxa3+ ({if} 32... c3+ 33. Qxc3 Qa2+ 34. Kc1) 33. Kxa3 f6 ({if
} 33... c3 34. Nd3 {intending Nc1-e2 or a2}) 34. exf6 {with a probable draw.})
32... Ke8 33. f5 ({A miscalculation, losing.} 33. g4 {should draw easily.})
33... exf5 34. e6 fxe6 35. Qe2 {The sealed move.} ({White had overlooked that
if} 35. Nxe6 c3+ 36. Kxc3 Qa3+ {forces the exchange of queens.}) 35... Kf7 36.
Qe5 Qd8 37. c3 Qc8 38. Qd6 (38. h4 {would make it more difficult for Black to
win.}) 38... Qc6 39. Qd8 Bh1 40. Nd7 (40. Qb8 {was better.}) 40... Qd6 {
Black wins the queen's side pawns. Notes by Mallison in his archives. Sources:
Tony Gillam, Bob Jones and H V Mallison archives.} (40... Qd6 {If} 41. Kc1 Bf3)
0-1

